= Evidence based belief in the effectiveness of Australia's new under 16 social media legislation \
#line(length: 100%)
#par()[
  Recently, the Australian government has introduced a new age verification law with the aim to protect Australian children that are under the age of sixteen years from the harmful qualities of unfiltered social platforms. This new law has sparked backlash amongst the Australian community with concerns primarily focused on end user privacy, social integrity and if it is even worth the enforcing effort. This paper aims to explore the morality of these new laws through the lens of William Clifford's ideology of belief only with sufficient evidence by performing an analysis on the agency backing these new laws.
]

#par()[
  On the tenth of December, 2025, the Australian government's world-first social media restriction for children under 16 years of age came into effect, which resulted in the access revocation of millions of online accounts nation wide. According to the eSafety Commissioner, the aim of the new restriction is to ensure that young Australians are protected from potentially harmful content by negating access to platforms that are designed to encourage maximal time spent on them. "Age-restricted social media platforms need to take reasonable steps to prevent Australians under [the age of] 16 years old from having accounts on their platforms @E2025Social". If the commissioner finds that a platform has not taken adequate steps to ensure the rigidity of the restrictions are upheld, there could be a penalty of up to 109.2 million Australian dollars. Before these regulations came into effect, there was major concern on how user's personal privacy would be protected in these new restrictions as many Australians disagreed with the notion of being tied to a single form of digital identification whenever they wanted to access the internet. To address this, three age assurance options were provided to companies, age verification, age estimation and age inference @Burgess2025The. 
Although the eSafety Commissioner has publicly made it clear that they strongly believe that a blanket age ban is the most effective way of protecting children online, organizations such as UNICEF and the Human Rights Law Centre strongly disagree @Shvartsman2025Safeguarding. Backlash has also sparked online as the general public voices their disdain for the new restrictions.
Given the beliefs of the two sides on the support spectrum of this new law, the obvious question of how effective these new regulations in protecting young Australians gets raised. In terms of the population of Australia, effectiveness of this new law is uncertain as it depends on the percentage of under-16s that were correctly banned versus the percentage of people that should not have been included, which is further dependant on the transparency of companies behind social media platforms. Another question "assuming that age-assurance methods are ineffective, how will that affect both adults and children in the long term" is also raised. If these methods are ineffective given the large population of the country, there will be a large portion of individuals who are on these platforms when they are not supposed to be. For example, if an adult wishes to use Instagram to become further integrated into a social circle they are in and is falsely deemed to be under sixteen years of age, they exclusively would have been robbed of that opportunity as other adults would not face the same treatment. Incidentally, if an Australian under the age of sixteen is falsely permitted access to social media platforms, they would be exposed to the full, unfiltered nature of the internet at an age where they haven't learnt to process that kind of information before which would have negative consequences in the child's development. In fact, this has already started to occur as many children are easily able to bypass the current implementations of age assurance by physically altering their appearance through different methods such as wearing facial makeup or by repeatedly verifying until they are let through @eSafetyCommissioner2026Social[p.~20].
]

#par()[
  William Clifford's perspective on the ethics of belief are that it is morally wrong for someone to believe anything based upon insufficient evidence and that everyone has a duty to question what others believe @William1879Lectures[p.~4]. In the context of the new child safety regulations placed to lock down social media accounts if the owner is determined to be under sixteen years of age - all public statements made by the eSafety Commissioner must be assumed to be truthful to determine the application of Clifford's beliefs. The eSafety Commissioner believes that by placing a blanket ban on all social media accounts owned by Australians under the age of sixteen, they are successful in protecting them from potential harmful content available online. However, Clifford expresses that by doing something unethical, even if you believe you are correct in your actions, you still violated a moral obligation that needs to be upheld by everyone. As an example, "If I steal money from any person, there may be no harm done from the mere transfer of possession ... [b]ut I cannot help doing this great wrong towards Man, that I make myself dishonest @William1879Lectures[p.~5]". Australians have the expectation to be "free" in common speech and online access, and the commissioner still has revoked a certain level of freedom from these young Australians. Clifford also expresses that if someone lets themselves believe anything on insufficient evidence, although no harm may have been done directly, actions were still taken with a lack of credibility, meaning that a moral injustice was committed to society. As this was a world-first legislation that makes use of cutting edge technology, the eSafety Commissioner has to ensure that they have a satisfactory amount of evidence in favour of the new law and it's effectiveness. Three months after legislation regarding child safety on social media was implemented, the eSafety Commissioner published a compliance update report outlining what the commission is aware of at the time of writing. "Between the 19th of January 2026 and the 2nd of February 2026, the eSafety Commissioner surveyed 898 parents and carers of children aged 8 to 15 years @eSafetyCommissioner2026Social". It was found that given half of the parents who stated their child had an account on at least one platform prior to legislation coming into effect, it was decreased to just over 30% of children having an account @eSafetyCommissioner2026Social. It is also understood by the eSafety Commissioner that "a substantial proportion of children under 16 retained accounts on age-restricted platforms @eSafetyCommissioner2026Social" meaning that overall, not much has changed from before legislation was introduced to after. This has the implication that the Commissioner believes that their legislation is working by publicly stating facts and figures when their own assessment proves otherwise, which implies that the commissioner is morally incorrect in their belief under Clifford's argument. For the eSafety Commissioner to undo this moral injustice, real evidence needs to be retrieved and reflected in future legislation.
]

#par()[
  If Clifford's advice and fundamental methodology on beliefs were applied to the public contention regarding online child safety regulations from the beginning, more evidence originating from the groups that oppose the new laws would have been considered. So far, all official information released by the eSafety Commissioner had been repeatedly saying to the public that they strongly believe these new laws will help in digital safety while many Australians refuse to believe them. The simple fact remains that the eSafety Commissioner believed their evidence was more credible than many groups of Australians. Even when the case where the eSafety Commissioner was successful in blocking social media access to all Australians under the age of sixteen with a one hundred percent success rate is considered, the implication is that they were successful in their persuit for total online safety, they still disrupted the Australian social contract of being digitally free, which still is a wrongdoing for the general public. The question gets raised then, is there any way for the eSafety Commissioner to maintain a level of online protection while maintaining the Australian social contract, making Clifford's advice to situations similar to this reasonable? The simplest answer raised by the general public is that the government should not be involving themselves in how parents raise their children in the first place. These groups of Australians are of the strong mindset that the government should help educate parents on how to protect their children in online spaces, but must not interfere in the affairs of Australians' online activity.
]

#par()[
  Is Clifford's position on the morality of evidence based belief reasonable? Let it be known as it is an important fact that William Clifford was a mathematician in parallel to being a philosopher. In mathematics a very big emphasis is put on logical proofs and absolute certainty as it is inherently deterministic. It is simple to say for example, event P is true if and only if event Q is true as and be certain that it will always hold. Philosophy in the realm of ethics is different however, where everything is subjective and will change over time. Therefore, attempting to a apply a hard rule and associating different scenarios to be black and white simply isn't feasable. For this reason, it is fair to say that Clifford's ideal model surrounding the ethics of belief may be true in some contexts as his famous quote "it is wrong always, everywhere, and for anyone, to believe anything upon insufficient evidence" is vague in the scope of what defines sufficient evidence. When this model is observed through the lens of the recent social media age restrictions, sufficient evidence in favour of either argument in support or rejection is implied to be decided democratically through whichever party has the best argument, which is not what has been observed in reality.
]

